<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iâl</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iâl, anglicised as Yale, was a medieval commote in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, around Llanarmon-yn-Iâl and the Alyn valley. Part of Powys Fadog, it passed after the English conquest to the marcher lordship of Bromfield and Yale. The Yale family, whose member Elihu Yale gave his name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale University</a>, took its name from the district.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ial.jpg" alt="Iâl" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Print_(BM_1878,0511.441).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1911 Hiram Bingham, a <b>Yale</b> lecturer seeking the last Inca capital, was led there by the farmer Melchor Arteaga; at the top two Quechua farmers, Richarte and Alvarez, were working terraces they had cleared four years before, and Richarte's 11-year-old son, Pablito, showed him the ruins.</blockquote><a href="/work/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Its tune was also borrowed for gentler ends: <b>Yale</b>'s &quot;Bright College Years&quot; (1881) and the school song of Doshisha University.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward Chiera published further tablets in 1924 and 1934, and others were found in the Louvre, the Ashmolean in Oxford, the Hilprecht collection at Jena, Geneva, Berlin, <b>Yale</b>, the Nippur collection in Istanbul, and at Ur itself in Charles Woolley's excavations.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1884, too, a variant with different words appeared as &quot;Cockles and Mussels&quot; in Students' Songs, a collection of songs sung at Harvard, <b>Yale</b>, Columbia and other American colleges.</blockquote><a href="/songs/molly-malone">Molly Malone</a></li><li><blockquote>Guided by local farmer Melchor Arteaga, <b>Yale</b> lecturer Hiram Bingham climbed to Machu Picchu, already known to local families.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-24">24 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It later settled in New Haven and in 1718 was renamed Yale College after the benefactor Elihu <b>Yale</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
